From 77e7324abc38055cb16aebf7a95e03226b626f15 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.local>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 22:23:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] merge_docket starved the judge for four hours, and the guard
 could not see a pass it started inside

TWO FIXES, one commit, because the seal costs 7-25 minutes to recover and both are small.

MERGE_DOCKET GETS ITS OWN CLOCK. Measured 2026-09-24: it last ran at 16:20:22Z and was still the
only thing standing between the unrunnable bank and the judge FOUR HOURS later -- rank 33 of 555
on staleness. It is a CORE leg whose measured cost runs to 721 s against a 2,640 s pass budget,
so the rotation crowded it out, and the bank it feeds (144,384 rows, 107,213 of them build
failures the judge re-attempted every single pass) reached nothing at all. Pre-warm failures over
the four passes in that window ran 81,588 -> 88,246 -> 109,792 -> 93,320: getting WORSE while the
fix sat one unrun leg away. It now has MT5-MergeDocket every 30 minutes, and its first run
filtered 34,484 rows out of the docket the judge reads (`unrunnable_filtered_from_docket`).

A CLOCK IS NOT A CUT: nothing leaves the rotation, no lane narrows, the leg gains capacity it
never had, and the ~721 s it used to take goes back to the core pass for the legs that stayed.
This is the remedy the desk already applied to state_vector and external_gauntlet. The test
pins that a name in OWN_CLOCK_LEGS must be out of every plan -- a name added without a task
stops the leg dead, so the task was registered and proven first.

THE GUARD REPORTED ZERO PASSES BESIDE TWO COMPLETED ONES. Root-caused rather than guessed: the
reader, the classifier and the fold all verify correct against ground truth (37 of 37 markers,
and a planted pass survives a round trip). The defect was the COLD START. It began 2 MB from the
end of a log the judge writes ~24 MB of per pass, so a fresh cursor landed INSIDE a pass --
after its PRE-WARM and before its epilogue -- and `fold` then correctly skipped an epilogue with
no pass to attach it to. The same thing happens after any outage, and there was one: an adoption
deleted this file for 38 minutes while the log advanced past a boundary.

The lookback is now 120 MB (~5 passes, ~1 s, paid once per cold start and never on the
incremental path), and a cold start EMITS A MARKER that reaches the artifact as `ledger_note`
and `cold_start_this_run`. That second half is the real fix: a zero that cannot be told apart
from "no passes happened" is what let this stand, and an instrument nobody can audit is worse
in a guard than anywhere else.

